lesson chemistry-minerals-and-hard-water v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Minerals dissolved in water, and hard water"
  goal "Measure how much mineral a water holds, say how hard it is, and explain where the minerals came from."

  look {
    scene 30x12 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=end {
        waterwheel mill paddles=8 lit=0
        stalactites st pairs=3 joined=1 rock=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Rain is a weak acid. As it soaks down through the limestone of the gorge it dissolves a little
      of the rock, so the water in the mill's well carries calcium and magnesium you cannot see.
      That is hard water. It makes soap turn to scum, it leaves limescale in the kettle, and where
      it drips in the cave behind the waterfall it builds stalactites. Hardness counts the calcium
      and magnesium as chalk: water with up to 60 mg of it in a litre is soft; over 180 mg is very
      hard.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chem.hardness-table count=1 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked chem.hard-water-left w=0 m=250 n=2 level=medium
    }
    practice chem.hard-water-left count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=11 hard-seed=4
    practice chem.kettle-fizz count=1 seed=1
    practice chem.soap-drops count=2 seed=1 hard-seed=4
    level hard {
      show chem.hard-water-left w=1 m=150 n=8
    }
  }
  story {
    show chem.stalactite-years w=0 n=3 easy-n=2 hard-w=1 hard-n=4
    show chem.stalactite-years w=1 n=2 easy-w=0 easy-n=3 hard-n=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.why-hard k=0 r=1 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.mix-soft m=180 easy-m=120 hard-m=240
  }
  remember {
    say "Hard water carries dissolved minerals from the rock. Boil it dry and they stay as a crust; limescale fizzes away in an acid."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that water carries dissolved minerals, measured in milligrams in each litre, and
    that water which has run through limestone or chalk is hard because rain, a weak acid, dissolves
    a little of the rock. The scale is the US Geological Survey's, which measures the calcium and
    magnesium only, counted as calcium carbonate (0 to 60 mg a litre soft, 61 to 120 moderately
    hard, 121 to 180 hard, over 180 very hard); other dissolved salts such as common salt do not
    make water hard, so the questions say calcium and magnesium where they use the scale. Limescale
    is the same calcium carbonate as chalk and limestone, left when hard water is heated, and
    vinegar fizzes it away. The soap drops are one child's results, not published figures.
    Stalactites grow on average about 0.13 mm a year, so about 13 cm in 1000 years, though some grow
    much faster. The wrong idea to watch for is that clear water has nothing in it. The three-star
    question dilutes the well water with rain until it is soft, where the minerals are shared over
    every litre, the well's own litre included. If the child multiplies when they should divide,
    stay here another day. Off the page: look inside a kettle, and with a grown-up soak a scaled
    spoon in vinegar overnight.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the crust from 2 litres is worked once in the teacher's pen, the pans hold 2 or 3 litres, limescale is named, the soap drops compare the stream and the rain, the stalactites are worked forward, the two-star asks why the spring is hard, and the three-star well water has 120 mg a litre."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the crust is worked back to the litres, the fizz's gas is named, the soap drops compare the well before and after boiling, the stalactites are worked back to their age, the two-star compares a limestone valley and a granite one, and the three-star well water has 240 or 300 mg a litre."
  }
}
